drumee-bootstrap (2.0.14) stable; urgency=medium

  * entrypoint/schemas init now populates as well as bootstraps, because "initialise the
    database" means both halves — natively bin/install runs the bootstrap and then
    populate.js. A schema with no sys_conf, no entity pool and no system accounts is not
    a usable instance: the application answers 500 with
    `call undefined.get_fonts_faces()`, which is what an empty sys_conf looks like from
    the outside.
    .
    It runs setup-schemas' OWN populate, not a container fork. That became possible with
    drumee-schemas 2.7.4, which taught lib/utils.js and lib/schema.js to pass the
    connection flags from db.json instead of assuming a local socket — so one populate
    serves both channels, which is the point of docs/channel-parity.md.
    .
    Idempotent: an instance whose yp.sys_conf already has rows is left alone. Re-running
    would mint new system accounts and a second entity pool every time a run-once job
    restarted.

 -- packaging@drumee.com  Mon, 10 Aug 2026 11:18:03 +0200

drumee-bootstrap (2.0.13) stable; urgency=medium

  * entrypoint/app starts pm2 the way the native channel does, because pm2-runtime
    cannot be used here. As PID 1 its argv is inherited by pm2's cluster wrapper, so
    every main/service worker received the ecosystem path as its OWN argv and Drumee's
    argparse rejected it: `ProcessContainer.js: error: unrecognized arguments: ...`,
    sixteen unstable restarts, then "errored" — while the container reported running and
    the web role answered 502.
    .
    Four invocations were eliminated by measurement, not by reading docs: `start <file>`,
    the bare `<file>`, a .js wrapper around it, and --no-daemon (2.0.6). Fork-mode apps
    survive the extra argument; cluster-mode ones do not, which is why `main` looked
    healthy while the entire REST tier was dead.
    .
    So it runs the same `pm2 start <ecosystem>` that /usr/sbin/drumee runs natively and
    holds PID 1 with `pm2 logs --raw`. The daemon spawns workers with no CLI argv to
    inherit. It also fails fast if anything is errored after eight seconds, because a
    role whose REST tier is dead otherwise reports healthy.
    .
    The underlying fault is not pm2's: an application should not die because its
    supervisor added an argument. parse_known_args in the Drumee apps would let
    pm2-runtime work directly, and that is a server-team change.

 -- somanos.sar@drumee.com  Sat, 08 Aug 2026 18:12:44 +0200

drumee-bootstrap (2.0.12) stable; urgency=medium

  * entrypoint/app: pm2-runtime takes the ecosystem file and nothing else. With `start`
    in front of it, pm2 passes "start <file>" down to each application as its OWN argv
    — and the Drumee processes parse argv, so every one of them exited with
    `ProcessContainer.js: error: unrecognized arguments: start
    /etc/drumee/infrastructure/ecosystem.json` and pm2 gave up after 16 unstable
    restarts, while the container itself reported running. The bare form brings main,
    main/service and factory online; verified before changing the package.
    .
    That is the second correction to this one line (2.0.6 removed --no-daemon), and
    both were the same mistake: assuming a pm2 invocation instead of running it.

